      "latency_ms": 5458.6,
      "prompt_tokens": 25,
      "completion_tokens": 1126,
      "output": "\n\nWater is a chemical substance with the molecular formula **H₂O**, consisting of two hydrogen atoms bonded to one oxygen atom. It is a fundamental component of life and one of the most abundant and versatile substances on Earth. Here's a breakdown of its key characteristics:\n\n### **Chemical and Physical Properties**\n- **Molecular Structure**: Water molecules are **polar**, meaning they have a slight positive charge on the hydrogen side and a slight negative charge on the oxygen side. This polarity allows water to form **hydrogen bonds** between molecules, giving it unique properties like high surface tension, cohesion, and adhesion.\n- **States of Matter**: Water exists as a **liquid** at standard temperature and pressure (room temperature), but it can also transition into a **solid** (ice) when frozen or a **gas** (water vapor) when heated. These phase changes are critical to Earth's **hydrological cycle**.\n- **Unique Properties**: \n  - **High specific heat capacity**: Water absorbs and releases heat slowly, helping regulate temperature in organisms and environments.\n  - **Solvent capabilities**: It dissolves many substances, earning it the title of \"universal solvent,\" which is vital for biological processes like nutrient transport.\n  - **Cohesion and adhesion**: Molecules stick to each other (cohesion) and to other surfaces (adhesion), enabling phenomena like capillary action in plants.\n\n### **Biological Importance**\n- **Essential for Life**: All known forms of life depend on water. It serves as a **medium for biochemical reactions**, a **transporter of nutrients and waste**, and a **component of cells, tissues, and bodily fluids** (e.g., blood, cytoplasm).\n- **Metabolic Role**: Water participates in **hydrolysis** (breaking down molecules) and **dehydration synthesis** (building molecules), which are central to metabolism.\n\n### **Environmental Role**\n- **Hydrological Cycle**: Water cycles through evaporation, condensation, precipitation, and runoff, shaping weather, climate, and ecosystems.\n- **Geological Impact**: It erodes rocks, shapes landscapes (e.g., rivers, canyons), and influences soil and sediment dynamics.\n\n### **Other Key Traits**\n- **Appearance**: Pure water is **colorless, odorless, and tasteless**. However, large bodies of water (like oceans) appear blue due to light absorption and scattering.\n- **Abundance**: Water covers about **71% of Earth's surface** (primarily in oceans) and is found in glaciers, groundwater, and the atmosphere.\n\nIn summary, water is a simple yet extraordinary molecule whose unique properties make it indispensable for life, environmental stability, and countless natural and industrial processes."
